# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accepted coverage floor: TicketNetwork, 16 games.
#
# WHAT IS ACCEPTED. This source alternates between serving 29 of 44 home games and 16,
# on the same GitHub runner, for the same URL. The full series:
#
#   09-05: 29 (residential)  09-06: 16  09-07: 16  09-08: 29
#   09-11: 29  09-12: 29  09-13: 16  09-14: 16
#
# The first low run is two days and CLOSED - 29 on both sides of it. The second is
# 09-13..09-14, which is still OPEN at the time of writing, so it is at least two days and
# its true length is unknown. Run length has therefore been measured ONCE at 2 and once at
# >= 2; the level has been measured on all eight observation days. That asymmetry is the
# whole argument below, so it is stated rather than rounded to "twice, both times 2".
#
# What it establishes is enough: COVERAGE_DROP_PERSIST_DAYS was set to 2 on 2026-09-13 as
# "the smallest window that distinguishes a flap from a step", against a series that
# ALREADY contained the closed two-day 09-06..09-07 run. The threshold was refuted by the
# data in hand when it was written, and it went red on 2026-09-14 on the flap it was built
# to tolerate.
#
# WHY A LEVEL RATHER THAN A LONGER DURATION. Raising the constant to 3 is the same
# mistake with a bigger number - eight observation days cannot support a run-length
# threshold, and a four-day flap would go red again.
#
# WHY IT IS ACCEPTABLE AT ALL. TickPick and Gametime both cover 44/44, so TicketNetwork is
# supplementary - CLAUDE.md already records the coverage itself as accepted, not fixed. A
# residential proxy is not worth the 13 games.
#
# WHAT A FLOOR ACTUALLY EXEMPTS, stated precisely because the first version of this entry
# overstated it and a reviewer had to measure the difference.
#
# A floor excuses GAMES, not days. A bloc of at least COVERAGE_DROP_MIN_GAMES games that
# leaves between two adjacent observation days, on a day whose coverage is still at or
# above the floor, is the source's window moving. Those specific games stay excused until
# they are served again - or until a bloc returns without them, which ends the excuse for
# everything still missing. Nothing else is excused. On an excused day:
#
#   - a sustained coverage drop is a warning rather than fatal, however long it holds
#   - those games do not accumulate toward a per-game hole. THIS HALF IS LOAD-BEARING.
#     Without it the floor lasted exactly one observation day: the coverage gate fires
#     only on day two of a low run - its baseline is a sliding three-day lookback - and
#     from day three the thirteen absent games tripped GAME_GAP_DAYS instead. Measured
#     against this store before the fix: a 2-day low run gave 0 fatal findings and a
#     3-day run gave 13, so a longer flap went red HARDER than a short one.
#
# TWO EARLIER SHAPES OF THIS RULE WERE WRONG, both green-when-broken, and both keyed on a
# LEVEL rather than on the departure. Recorded because the level framing is the intuitive
# one and will be proposed again:
#
#   "below the series peak"      made every day after a source's best day excused, so one
#                                game quietly dropping was hidden.
#   "clears the drop gate        looks tighter and is not. The peak is a max over the whole
#    against the peak"           series and never decays, so a single 39-game day excused
#                                every later 29-game day for the rest of the season. And
#                                with peak 29 and floor 16 the excused levels are 16..21,
#                                which late-season attrition MUST walk through - about
#                                twelve consecutive days with per-game detection silently
#                                off, at the end of the season.
#
# A departure test has no peak and no window. Attrition retires about one game at a time,
# so it never forms a bloc and never excuses anything at any point in the season.
#
# That sentence was FALSE as first written, and the correction is the interesting part.
# Attrition retires one game per CALENDAR day, but the rule compares adjacent OBSERVATION
# days - and this project has observation gaps by design; this very file exists to accept
# them. Across a six-day outage about three games retire together, which clears
# COVERAGE_DROP_MIN_GAMES, and a genuinely dead game departing in the same interval rode
# along excused. Measured: a dead game caught with no floor was HIDDEN with one. Games
# already played are now filtered out of the departure diff, which makes the claim true as
# written rather than true-if-you-never-miss-a-day.
#
# THE SAFETY PROPERTY. A floor can only RELAX the per-game check, never tighten it, so it
# cannot manufacture a finding that would not exist without it. Brute-forced over 60,000
# random series: zero violations. That is the strongest guarantee this design offers, and
# it is what makes a floor safe to add for a source you have reviewed - the failure mode
# of getting one wrong is a missed finding, never a false alarm.
#
# WHAT IS STILL FATAL. Everything the check caught before, plus cases the level framing
# missed. Measured on this store with three sustained days:
#
#   collapse straight from 29:   to 15 -> 14 fatal findings, 12 -> 17, 11 -> 18, 5 -> 24
#   further collapse from the    to 15 ->  1 fatal finding,  12 ->  4, 11 ->  5, 5 -> 11
#     already-excused 16:
#
# The second row is smaller ON PURPOSE and the asymmetry is the design, not a weakness:
# the thirteen games that left at an accepted level stay explained, so what is reported is
# what is NEWLY unexplained. Note the coverage gate alone would catch none of the second
# row - 16 -> 12 clears neither its three-game nor its quarter threshold. The per-game
# check is what fires.
#
# Also still fatal, and NOT caught by either level-keyed version: a game dropped while the
# source sits inside its low mode, and a game that fails to return when its bloc does.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH. Why the source flaps. One run cannot separate a cache
# behind TicketNetwork's CDN from a genuine window change, and nothing here is evidence
# either way. If 16 ever becomes permanent this file will record it as accepted rather
# than flag it, which is the honest cost of accepting the level: the flap and a permanent
# step to 16 are indistinguishable to this check by construction.
#
# CONFIDENCE: measured, on 8 observation days for the level and 2 runs for the duration.
# The level is what this entry rests on; the duration is explicitly NOT relied upon.
#
# THE ALTERNATIVE NOT TAKEN, so the next session does not have to rediscover it. A
# high-water-mark rule - "has this source returned to 29 within the last N days?" - would
# distinguish a flap from a permanent step, which is the one capability accepting the
# level gives up. It was rejected because N is a duration threshold wearing different
# clothes, and the duration is the quantity this series cannot support.
#
# One qualification on that rejection, from review, because it softens the revisit bar:
# the two N's are not equally fragile. COVERAGE_DROP_PERSIST_DAYS must EXCEED the longest
# flap run, and its false positives are frequent and expensive - a red run on healthy data
# roughly every other day. A high-water-mark N must exceed the longest gap between high
# days, its false positives fall off as N grows, and the only cost of a generous N is
# detection latency on something that is not urgent: a permanent step on a supplementary
# source is not a data emergency. So N need only be BOUNDED generously, not fitted - three
# weeks would do. Revisit when there are enough closed runs to bound it; that needs weeks,
# not days, and do not re-reject it on the equivalence argument alone.
coverage ticketnetwork 16
